The current implementation of `sortOps` uses a manual $O(N^2)$ exchange sort. It is more idiomatic and efficient to use `sort.Slice` from the standard library, especially since this project uses Go 1.23. Note that you will need to add `"sort"` to your imports.

```go
func sortOps(ops []Op) {
	// Deterministic ordering: blocks first, then unblocks, MAC ascending.
	// Avoids spurious diffs in test fixtures and CI output.
	sort.Slice(ops, func(i, j int) bool {
		return less(ops[i], ops[j])
	})
}
